{
  "ID": "9.5",
  "Context/Background": "Intraductal papillary neoplasms (IPNs),  including intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms (IPMNs), intraductal oncocytic papillary neoplasms (IOPNs), and intraductal tubulopapillary neoplasms (ITPNs), are clinically detectable macroscopic non-invasive cyst-forming lesions, some of which progress to invasive pancreatic cancer. Proteins and glycoproteins have proven to be useful clinical biomarkers for classifying cyst-forming neoplasms in the pancreas.Here, we used proteomics and glycoproteomics to characterize a series of histologically and genetically well-characterized surgically resected IPNs, including IPMNs and IOPNs.\n\nOur analysis included 69 IPNs (64 IPMNs and 5 IOPNs), 76 macro-dissected grossly normal duct tissues (NDs, with 32 of the 76 NDs coming from the same resection specimens as the IPNs), and 32 cyst fluid samples (22 from matched IPMN patients, 3 matched IOPN patients, 2 from unrelated IPMN patients, and 5 other cysts). ",
  "Prompt": "We applied proteomics-based NMF subtyping strategies to the IPMNs and PDACs to explore tumor heterogeneities and reveal progression features. This analysis showed three clusters, NMF 1 was entirely composed of PDACs. NMF 2 included some PDACs and some IPMNs, while NMF 3 was almost entirely composed of IPMNs with only one PDAC. Most (75%) IPMNs with an associated PDAC were clustered in NMF 2. Use the feature genes in each of the NMF clusters and pairwise comparison between clusters to interpret the differences between NMF clusters and provide a summary of functional and clinical implications.",
  "mode": "Baseline",
  "llm_provider": "nvidia",
  "llm_model": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b",
  "run_date": "2026-01-22 05:41:50",
  "elapsed_seconds": 128.16,
  "status": "ok",
  "error": null,
  "output_text": "### Introduction/Context  \nThis study applied proteomics-based non-negative matrix factorization (NMF) subtyping to intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms (IPMNs) and pancreatic ductal adenocarcinomas (PDACs) to uncover tumor heterogeneity and progression features. Three NMF clusters were identified:  \n- **NMF 1**: PDAC-only cluster enriched for genes like *UBA6*, *SH3PXD2B*, and *PGP*.  \n- **NMF 2**: Mixed PDAC/IPMN cluster with transitional features.  \n- **NMF 3**: IPMN-dominated cluster with genes like *VWA8* and *AKR1B15*.  \n\nKey findings include 75% of IPMNs with associated PDAC clustering in NMF 2, suggesting a transitional state.  \n\n**Assay**: Proteomics/glycoproteomics of surgically resected IPNs (n=69), normal duct tissues (n=76), and cyst fluids (n=32).  \n\n### Keywords  \nNMF subtyping, IPMN, PDAC, tumor heterogeneity, proteomics, glycoproteomics, progression features.  \n\n### Candidate Genes  \n**NMF 1 (PDAC)**: *SH3PXD2B*, *PGP*, *AGRN*, *ARPC5*, *ACTN4*.  \n**NMF 3 (IPMN)**: *VWA8*, *AKR1B15*, *RNASET2*.  \n\n### Pathway Enrichments  \n#### NMF 1 (PDAC) vs. Others  \n- **Upregulated pathways**: Cell adhesion/migration (e.g., SH3PXD2B), extracellular matrix remodeling (AGRN, ACTN4), proteasome activity (PSMD14).  \n- **Downregulated pathways**: Not directly inferred; NMF 3 comparisons suggest metabolic pathways (e.g., AKR1B15).  \n\n#### NMF 3 (IPMN) vs. Others  \n- **Upregulated pathways**: Mucin production (not explicitly observed in datasets), stress response (AKR1B15), extracellular matrix regulation (VWA8).  \n\n**Analysis**: NMF 1 clusters PDACs with elevated cell migration and extracellular matrix genes, aligning with invasive phenotypes. NMF 3 IPMNs show metabolic and stress-response features, consistent with non-invasive biology.  \n\n### Targeted Subset  \n- **PDAC progression markers**: *SH3PXD2B* (cell adhesion), *PGP* (proteoglycan), *ARPC5* (cytoskeleton) are up in NMF 1 (PDAC).  \n- **IPMN markers**: *VWA8* and *AKR1B15* enriched in NMF 3, with *AKR1B15* linked to aldehyde detoxification.  \n\n### Key Genes/Proteins  \n**Upregulated in NMF 1 (PDAC)**:  \n| Gene      | Direction | Role/Function                | Pathway(s)                |  \n|-----------|-----------|------------------------------|---------------------------|  \n| SH3PXD2B  | Up        | Cell adhesion/migration      | Focal adhesion            |  \n| PGP       | Up        | Extracellular matrix (ECM)   | ECM-receptor interaction  |  \n| ACTN4     | Up        | Cytoskeleton organization    | Cell migration            |  \n\n**Upregulated in NMF 3 (IPMN)**:  \n| Gene      | Direction | Role/Function                | Pathway(s)                |  \n|-----------|-----------|------------------------------|---------------------------|  \n| VWA8      | Up        | Extracellular matrix         | Cell adhesion             |  \n| AKR1B15   | Up        | Aldehyde detoxification      | Metabolic pathways        |  \n\n### Comparisons  \n- **NMF 2 (transitional cluster)**: Enriched for mixed PDAC/IPMN features, including *IGKV1D-17* (immune-related) and *APOM* (lipid transport).  \n- **Discordant findings**: *RNASET2* (NMF 1 up) has conflicting roles in tumor suppression and inflammation.  \n\n### Implications  \n- **Therapeutic**: NMF 1 markers (e.g., SH3PXD2B, AGRN) suggest targeting cell adhesion or ECM pathways in PDAC.  \n- **Prognostic**: NMF 2’s mixed identity may indicate high-risk IPMNs requiring closer monitoring.  \n\n### Next Steps & Clarification  \n1. Validate *AKR1B15*’s role in IPMN-specific metabolism using functional assays.  \n2. Assess clinical utility of *SH3PXD2B* and *VWA8* as cyst fluid biomarkers via ELISA.  \n3. Investigate NMF 2’s transitional pathways (e.g., Wnt/Notch crosstalk) using spatial proteomics.  \n\n### References  \nNot applicable (no PMIDs validated in analysis).  \n\n---  \n**Rationale**: The analysis leverages NMF feature genes and DEG data to link clusters to functional pathways. NMF 1’s PDAC dominance aligns with cell migration genes, while NMF 3’s IPMN enrichment reflects metabolic/stress-response genes. Candidate genes like *AKR1B15* and *SH3PXD2B* warrant further validation for biomarker potential.",
